e and said how much do you give to  charity and I have taste yeah dude i'm  making a theoretical argument here but  but I thought about it and I didn't give  very much so I contacted um what's now  planned USA and asked him for  information about their charity and a  little while later this was before the  internet got so popular I got a package  from them and I remember open remember  opening up the package and expecting to  see pamphlets full of numbers and data  but they did something extraordinarily  clever they sent me a child they said  thank you for writing we know you're not  committed but if you choose to commit  here is a child whose life you will save  and they had a photograph and a letter  he wrote and a drawing of his house  where he lived in his village and it was  immensely persuasive we can we we are  wired up to be to be incited to kindness  by seeing people not numbers not  statistics um and this could have an  effect on our morality more generally  the philosopher Martha Nussbaum talks  about this in the context of Greek  tragedies so she says that although all  of the future citizens who saw age and  tragedies were male they were asked to  have to suffer to have empathy the  suffering of many whose law could never  be there's such as Trojans and Persians  and Africans such as wives and daughters  and mothers um what stories can do is  they could take the world from being an  undifferentiated mass movie to think  about them as specific individuals and  have you think about them as friends and  family I've talked about laboratory  studies that this happens but it most  likely happens in the real world as well  does anybody know who this is for me  harriet beecher stowe and harriet  beecher stowe uh was a novelist and this  was in America and it was right before  it was a during the time of slavery and  slavery according to slavery according  to many historians and many scholars was  not ended by by theological arguments by  religious arguments or 